// EXPECT: FAIL
// ERROR_CONTAINS: ANUBIS_ASSERTION_UNPROVEN
// Regression (design decision 2026-07-21, hunt wf_7451545b): an in-body `assert(P)` over an INTEGER
// PARAMETER of a NON-contract function was formerly param-opaque. The checker modeled parameters only
// for functions that DECLARE a `requires`/`ensures`, so here `x` was an unknown symbol, `x < 0` was
// not bool-modelable, and the assert was SILENTLY DROPPED — `check` passed while `run` traps (exit
// 101, ANUBIS_ASSERT_FAILED) on `f(5)`, a green check certifying what run violates. The middle-option
// models integer params for ANY function whose body asserts over them, so this genuinely-unprovable
// assert (`x` is an unconstrained i64; `5 < 0` is false) is now correctly REJECTED. The intended fix
// for a program that means it is to state the precondition as `requires(x < 0)`, which discharges it.
pub fn f(x: int) {
    assert(x < 0);
}

fn main() {
    f(5);
}
